--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -20,6 +20,22 @@ A file that cannot be read is reported on stderr as `tally: cannot read <path>`,
 the other files are still counted, and the exit code is 1. An unknown option
 prints a usage line on stderr and exits with code 2. Otherwise the exit code is 0.
 
+Pass `--json` (it may appear anywhere among the arguments) to get a single JSON
+document instead of the text table, e.g.
+
+```
+node bin/tally.js --json notes.txt todo.txt
+```
+
+prints `{"files":[...],"total":{...}}` followed by a newline and nothing else.
+`files` has one entry per file that was read, in command-line order, each shaped
+`{"path":"notes.txt","lines":3,"words":12,"chars":80}`; `total`
+sums `lines`, `words` and `chars` over those files and is always present, even
+for a single file. With no files at all the output is
+`{"files":[],"total":{"lines":0,"words":0,"chars":0}}`. Unreadable files behave
+as they do in the text mode: reported on stderr, omitted from `files`, and the
+exit code is 1.
+
 Code layout: `src/count.js` does the counting, `src/cli.js` parses arguments and
 prints (`run(argv, io)` takes the IO functions as parameters so it can be
 tested without touching the real terminal or disk), `bin/tally.js` wires it to
